  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   - The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem aims to find a step function h(x) that minimizes the maximum weighted overlap with its shifted versions.\n   - Our strategy involves three key phases: \n     1. Multi-resolution optimization to capture patterns at different scales\n     2. Penalized gradient-based refinement to handle non-convexity explicitly\n     3. Distributive initialization to escape local optima\n   - We use FFT-accelerated evaluations to compute the objective efficiently (2-3ms per n=512 evaluation)\n   - The non-convexity issue is intentionally addressed by incorporating sparse penalty terms and coordinate-wise search, rather than forcing convexity.\n   - This approach differs from previous local optimization by incorporating analytical penalty forms and multi-start distributed initialization.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Domain: [0,2] discretized into n_points samples\n   - Objective:\n      C_5(h) = (2/n_points) * max_k [ sum_i h[i]*(1-h[i+k(mod)]) ]\n   - Constraints:\n     1. Integral: sum(h) = n_points/2\n     2. Bounds: \u2200i, 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n   - Formulation:\n     Minimize_C_5: -(lower bound strategy still TODO)\n     exTerm(i,k): h[i]*(1-h[(i+k)mod])\n     We use FFT for rapid cross-convolution evaluation:\n        T_k = sum_i exTerm(i,k) = S - auto_correlation(k)\n        where S = \u222bh(x)dx, auto_correlation(k) = circular auto-convolution of h\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   Phase 0: Parent Validation and Up-scaling\n   - Validate parent solution at n=80 meets integral and bounding constraints\n   - Use interpolation to scale to arbitrary n_final with integer scaling factors\n   - Maintain integral constraint via post-sampling adjustment\n\n   Phase 1: Initialization and Search\n   - Generate L candidates with different:\n       A. Random local perturbations\n       B. Multi-rotation pattern propagation\n       C. Explicit penalty-enhanced functions\n   - All candidates constrained to: sum(h)=n_final/2\n\n   Phase 2: Progressive Resolution (if time/feasible)\n   - Start at n_final_0 \u2208 [1000, 1600] based on time budget\n   - Fall back to interpolation (n_approx=n_final_0)\n\n   Phase 3: Gradient-based Optimization\n   - Use SciPy.minimize(method='SLSQP') with:\n        fun: Fast numpy.correlate (mode='full') implementation to avoid FFT complexity\n        jac: Analytical gradient with special handling for wrapping effects\n        Constraints: Integral + box constraints via bounds\n   - Incorporate separable penalties:\n        L_\u03c8 = \u2211_{i} \u03c8(h[i]), where\n          \u03c8[x] = 0 if x\u2208[0,1] else (1-x) or (x-0) whichever positive\n\n   Phase 4: Stalling Detection and Grid Redesign\n   - For each grid: Track best 'ghost' candidate when no improvement\n   - Alternate strain function between lags and fixed-interaction patterns\n   - If progress stalls: Vary lag=1 and lag=2 cross-terms + diversify by perturbation\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\n   - Default n_final: 1200 (greatest efficiency for 720s execution)\n   - Candidate variants (30% of budget):\n        1. Uniform random ensamble (10%)\n        2. Binary representation (20%)\n        3. Segment-interlaced structured (30%)\n   - Optimization variants (70% budget):\n        1. Equality-constrained method ('SLSQP') default\n        2. Amplitude-constrained projection when near boundaries (optional but recommended)\n        3. Lag-specific penalty scaling (adjust penalty factors by lag)\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   - Baseline cost: \u223c1 evaluation every 3ms at n=1200\n   - Default budget: 100,000 evaluations \u2248 300s + surrogates\n   - Critical timing points: \n        a. Just before entering optimization loops\n        b. During ghost candidate validation\n        c. Near convergence (10^6 evals)\n   - Handling:\n        \u221a Memory for evolving \u22482000\u00d71_PROJ (each 1.2kb) \u2248 240MB manageable in 16GB systems\n        \u221a Warm-start with best ghost candidate on stall/diversions\n        \u221a Fallback to D-optimality if SLSQP fails\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - Candidate test:\n        1. 0<=h[i]<=1 (floating point compatibility)\n        2. Integral: |sum(h)| < 1e-5 of n_final/2\n        3. C_5 eval: np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full') without normalization\n        4. Recovery: Fall to closest ghost candidate if stuck\n   - Return values:\n        h_numpy_array: satisfies constraints\n        c5_bound: scalar float of computed max overlap\n        n_points: integer sample size\n\nDetailed function breakdown implemented in subsequent phases\nFor brevity, only core structures below:",
